@node Naming system
@section Naming system

In FORTRAN, variable names use @code{case-insensitive} naming (conventionally @code{UPPER_CASE} or @code{mixed_case}).
All keywords are case-insensitive but conventionally written in uppercase.

@table @asis
@item Keywords: PROGRAM, SUBROUTINE, FUNCTION, END, IF, THEN, ELSE, ENDIF, DO, CONTINUE, ENDDO, CALL, RETURN, PAUSE, STOP, READ, WRITE, PRINT, FORMAT, DIMENSION, COMMON, EQUIVALENCE, DATA, INTEGER, REAL, DOUBLE PRECISION, COMPLEX, LOGICAL, CHARACTER, PARAMETER, EXTERNAL, INTRINSIC
@item Operators: +, -, *, /, **, =, .EQ., .NE., .LT., .LE., .GT., .GE., .NOT., .AND., .OR., .EQV., .NEQV.
@item String delimiters: Single quotes (@code{'...'}) or double quotes (@code{"..."}) for character literals
@item Comment lines: Indicated by @code{C} or @code{*} in column 1, or @code{!} anywhere after column 6
@end table

@node Basic structure
@section Basic structure for a stand-alone routine or program

A FORTRAN program consists of a main program and optional subroutines and functions.

@example
       PROGRAM GAMELOGIC
       IMPLICIT NONE
       INTEGER :: SCORE = 0
       INTEGER :: LIVES_LEFT = 3
       INTEGER :: CURRENT_ROOM = 1

       CALL INITIALIZE_GAME()
       DO WHILE (.NOT. GAME_OVER)
           CALL PROCESS_INPUT()
           CALL UPDATE_GAME()
           CALL RENDER_DISPLAY()
       END DO
       STOP
       END PROGRAM GAMELOGIC

       SUBROUTINE INITIALIZE_GAME()
       SCORE = 0
       LIVES_LEFT = 3
       CURRENT_ROOM = 1
       END SUBROUTINE INITIALIZE_GAME

       SUBROUTINE PROCESS_INPUT()
       CHARACTER(20) :: PLAYER_INPUT
       READ(*, '(A)') PLAYER_INPUT
       IF (PLAYER_INPUT .EQ. 'MOVE NORTH') THEN
           CURRENT_ROOM = CURRENT_ROOM + 10
       END IF
       END SUBROUTINE PROCESS_INPUT

       SUBROUTINE UPDATE_GAME()
       ! Game logic updates
       END SUBROUTINE UPDATE_GAME

       SUBROUTINE RENDER_DISPLAY()
       ! Rendering code
       END SUBROUTINE RENDER_DISPLAY
@end example

@node Basic structure object
@section Basic structure for an object method

FORTRAN does not have built-in object-oriented programming, but objects can be simulated using derived types and type-bound procedures.

@example
       TYPE :: PLAYER_TYPE
           INTEGER :: X = 0
           INTEGER :: Y = 0
           INTEGER :: SPRITE = 0
       END TYPE PLAYER_TYPE

       TYPE(PLAYER_TYPE), PUBLIC :: PLAYER

       INTERFACE
           MODULE SUBROUTINE MOVE_PLAYER(SELF, DX, DY)
               IMPORT :: PLAYER_TYPE
               TYPE(PLAYER_TYPE), INTENT(INOUT) :: SELF
               INTEGER, INTENT(IN) :: DX, DY
           END SUBROUTINE MOVE_PLAYER
       END INTERFACE

       CONTAINS

       MODULE SUBROUTINE MOVE_PLAYER(SELF, DX, DY)
           TYPE(PLAYER_TYPE), INTENT(INOUT) :: SELF
           INTEGER, INTENT(IN) :: DX, DY
           SELF%X = SELF%X + DX
           SELF%Y = SELF%Y + DY
       END SUBROUTINE MOVE_PLAYER
@end example

@node Calling routines
@section Calling a routine (nullary)

To call a subroutine that takes no arguments:

@example
       CALL INITIALIZE_GRAPHICS()
       CALL CLEAR_SCREEN()
       CALL PLAY_STARTUP_MUSIC()
@end example

@node Calling routines value
@section Calling a routine with a value (in accumulator)

To call a function that returns a single value:

@example
       SCORE = CALCULATE_BONUS(LEVEL, TIME_LEFT)
       BONUS = GET_INPUT_FACTOR()
       VOLUME = GET_MASTER_VOLUME()
@end example

@node Calling routines giving
@section Calling a routine giving a value (in accumulator)

Not applicable in FORTRAN's standard calling convention.

@node Calling routines values
@section Calling a routine with values in and out (in accumulator)

To call a subroutine with parameters:

@example
       CALL DRAW_SPRITE(SPRITE_ID, XPOS, YPOS)
       CALL PLAY_SOUND(SOUND_ID, VOLUME, LOOP_FLAG)
       CALL CHECK_COLLISION(OBJ1, OBJ2, RESULT_VAR)
@end example

@node Calling library
@section Calling a library routine (all 4 styles)

FORTRAN accesses library routines through standard CALL statements or function invocations:

@example
       CALL LOAD_LEVEL(LEVEL_NUMBER)          @r{Direct call}
       CALL FAR_PROC(LIBRARY_ADDRESS)         @r{Far call}
       RESULT = LIBRARY_FUNCTION(INPUT)       @r{Library function}
       CALL OBJECT%METHOD(PARAM1, PARAM2)     @r{Object method call}
@end example

These correspond to:
@table @asis
@item Direct: CALL ProcedureName(...)
@item Far: CALL AbsoluteAddress(...)
@item Library: CALL LibraryEntry(...) or FunctionName(...)
@item Object: OBJECT%MethodName(...)
@end table

@node Calling far bank
@section Calling a far bank Service (all 4 styles)

Far procedures in FORTRAN are called using absolute addresses or through
INTERFACE blocks that specify the memory location:

@example
       INTERFACE
           SUBROUTINE FAR_LOAD_LIBRARY(ADDR)
               INTEGER, INTENT(IN) :: ADDR
           END SUBROUTINE FAR_LOAD_LIBRARY
       END INTERFACE

       CALL FAR_LOAD_LIBRARY(INT(Z'8000'))   @r{Call routine in bank 1}
@end example

These correspond to:
@table @asis
@item Direct: Not used for far calls
@item Far: CALL with interface specifying far address
@item Library: Not applicable
@item Object: Not applicable
@end table

@node Invoking object
@section Invoking a method on an object (all 4 styles)

Object method invocation in FORTRAN uses the type-bound procedure syntax:

@example
       CALL PLAYER%MOVE_PLAYER(5, 10)
       CALL SCORE_DISPLAY%UPDATE(SCORE)
       GAME_OVER_FLAG = GAME_STATE%IS_GAME_OVER()
@end example

These correspond to:
@table @asis
@item Direct: Setting object properties via derived type assignment
@item Far: Not applicable for methods (objects typically in same memory)
@item Library: Method may call library routines internally
@item Object: TYPE-BOUND PROCEDURE invocation on object instance
@end table

@node Assignment
@section Assigning to a variable

Variables are assigned using the assignment operator (=):

@example
       SCORE = 0                           @r{Explicit assignment}
       LIVES_LEFT = 3                      @r{Implicit assignment}
       COUNTER = COUNTER + 1               @r{Increment}
       LIVES_LEFT = LIVES_LEFT - 1         @r{Decrement}
       SCORE = SCORE + 10                  @r{Arithmetic assignment}
       MESSAGE = 'Hello, World!'           @r{String assignment}
@end example

@node Assignment object
@section Assigning to an object slot

Object slots (derived type components) are assigned using the component selector (%):

@example
       PLAYER%X = 160                      @r{Set object's x coordinate}
       PLAYER%Y = 100                      @r{Set object's y coordinate}
       PLAYER%SPRITE = 5                   @r{Set sprite number}
       CALL PLAYER%SET_VISIBLE(.TRUE.)     @r{Set visibility flag}
@end example

@node Assignment array
@section Assigning to an array member with a subscript

Array elements are assigned using parentheses for indexing:

@example
       INTEGER, DIMENSION(10) :: SCORE_TABLE
       CHARACTER(3), DIMENSION(10) :: INITIALS
       INTEGER, DIMENSION(8,8) :: BOARD

       SCORE_TABLE(1) = 100
       INITIALS(1) = 'ALI'
       BOARD(3,4) = 1
@end example

@node Arithmetic
@section Basic arithmetic: addition and subtraction

Basic arithmetic uses the + and - operators:

@example
       SCORE = SCORE + 10                  @r{Addition}
       LIVES_LEFT = LIVES_LEFT - 1         @r{Subtraction}
       TOTAL = WIDTH + HEIGHT              @r{Add two variables}
       FINAL_PRICE = PRICE - DISCOUNT      @r{Subtraction}
       DELTA = NEW_VALUE - OLD_VALUE       @r{Subtraction}
@end example

@node Arithmetic bit
@section Basic arithmetic: bit-shifting for mutiplication or division

Bit-shifting for multiplication/division by powers of two is not standard in FORTRAN 77.
Use multiplication/division or the IBITS, ISHFT, etc. intrinsics in later standards:

@example
       RESULT = VALUE * 4                  @r{Multiply by 2^2}
       RESULT = VALUE / 2                  @r{Divide by 2^1}
       RESULT = VALUE * 8                  @r{Multiply by 2^3}
       RESULT = VALUE / 16                 @r{Divide by 2^4}
       ! Using ISHFT (Fortran 90+)
       RESULT = ISHFT(VALUE, 2)            @r{Left shift 2 bits (multiply by 4)}
       RESULT = ISHFT(VALUE, -1)           @r{Right shift 1 bit (divide by 2)}
@end example

@node Complex arithmetic
@section Complex arithmetic: multiple expressions

Complex arithmetic expressions follow standard precedence rules:

@example
       TOTAL = (BASE_SALARY * 12) + BONUS - DEDUCTIONS
       AVERAGE = (SUM1 + SUM2 + SUM3) / 3.0
       DISTANCE = SQRT((X2 - X1)**2 + (Y2 - Y1)**2)
@end example

@node Boolean
@section Boolean expressions: equality and inequalities

Boolean expressions use logical operators and relation operators:

@example
       IF (SCORE .GT. 1000) THEN ...         @r{Greater than}
       IF (SCORE .LT. 50) THEN ...           @r{Less than}
       IF (SCORE .EQ. 100) THEN ...          @r{Equality}
       IF (SCORE .NE. 0) THEN ...            @r{Inequality}
       IF (X .GE. 0 .AND. X .LT. MAX_X) THEN ... @r{Combined conditions}
       IF (BUTTON .EQ. BUTTON_A .OR. BUTTON .EQ. BUTTON_B) THEN ... @r{Alternative conditions}
@end example

@node If then else
@section Using if/then/else structures and examine/cond structures

Conditional execution uses IF/THEN/ELSE/END IF blocks:

@example
       IF (TEMPERATURE .GT. THOT) THEN
           STATUS = 'HOT'
       ELSE IF (TEMPERATURE .GT. TCOLD) THEN
           STATUS = 'WARM'
       ELSE
           STATUS = 'COLD'
       END IF

       SELECT CASE (ANSWER)
       CASE ('Y', 'y')
           RESPONSE = 'YES'
       CASE ('N', 'n')
           RESPONSE = 'NO'
       CASE DEFAULT
           RESPONSE = 'INVALID'
       END SELECT
@end example

FORTRAN does not have an EXAMINE/COND structure; SELECT CASE serves
a similar purpose for multi-way branching.

@node Performing
@section Performing loops

Loops are implemented using DO loops with various forms:

@example
       ! DO loop equivalent (known iterations)
       DO I = 1, 10
           PRINT *, 'Count: ', I
       END DO

       ! DO WHILE loop equivalent (pre-test)
       I = 1
       DO WHILE (I .LE. 10)
           PRINT *, 'Count: ', I
           I = I + 1
       END DO

       ! Infinite loop with EXIT
       DO
           READ(*, '(A)') COMMAND
           IF (COMMAND .EQ. 'QUIT') EXIT
           CALL PROCESS_COMMAND(COMMAND)
       END DO

       ! DO loop with EXIT and CYCLE
       DO I = 1, 100
           IF (ARRAY(I) .EQ. TARGET_VALUE) THEN
               PRINT *, 'Found at index: ', I
               EXIT
           END IF
           IF (ARRAY(I) .LT. 0) THEN
               CYCLE
           END IF
           ! Process positive values
       END DO
@end example

@node Breaking loops
@section Breaking out of loops safely

Early loop exit is achieved using EXIT or CYCLE statements:

@example
       ! Exit DO loop early
       DO I = 1, 100
           IF (ARRAY(I) .EQ. TARGET_VALUE) THEN
               PRINT *, 'Found at index: ', I
               EXIT                    @r{Exit loop immediately}
           END IF
       END DO

       ! Skip current iteration
       DO I = 1, 100
           IF (ARRAY(I) .LT. 0) THEN
               CYCLE                   @r{Skip to next iteration}
           END IF
           ! Process non-negative values
       END DO

       ! Nested loop control
       OUTER: DO I = 1, 10
           INNER: DO J = 1, 10
               IF (MATRIX(I,J) .EQ. 0) THEN
                   EXIT INNER          @r{Exit inner loop only}
               END IF
               ! Process non-zero elements
           END DO INNER
       END DO OUTER
@end example

@node Dialogue
@section Calling a dialogue script (Forth scripts)

FORTRAN does not directly call Forth dialogue scripts. Instead, it uses
its own procedures. However, through the semantic-sugar
layer, FORTRAN commands can be mapped to calls of the shared Fountain/Forth
runtime library:

@example
       ! Original FORTRAN code
       IF ((X .GT. 100) .AND. (X .LT. 200)) THEN
           CALL SHOW_MESSAGE(MSG_WELCOME)
       END IF

       ! Desugared via semantic-sugar layer
       IF ((X > 100) .AND. (X < 200)) THEN
           (:call-acc :target "ShowWelcomeMessage" :using nil)
       END IF
@end example

@node Tiles
@section Manipulating tiles

Tile manipulation is performed through direct video memory access
or through library routines for tiled backgrounds:

@example
       ! Direct memory access (mode-dependent)
       VIDEO_SEGMENT = Z'B800'
       OFFSET = ROW * 320 + COL
       POKE VIDEO_SEGMENT, OFFSET*2, TILE_NUM
       POKE VIDEO_SEGMENT, OFFSET*2+1, ATTRIBUTE

       ! Library routine approach
       CALL LOAD_TILESET(TILESET_ID)
       CALL PLACE_TILE(LAYER, X, Y, TILE_INDEX)
       CALL SCROLL_BACKGROUND(DX, DY)
@end example

@node Decals
@section Manipulating decals

Decals (sprites or movable objects) are manipulated using:

@example
       ! Hardware sprite approach (if available)
       CALL INIT_SPRITES
       CALL SET_SPRITE_POSITION(SPRITE_ID, X, Y)
       CALL SET_SPRITE_IMAGE(SPRITE_ID, IMAGE_ID)
       CALL SHOW_SPRITE(SPRITE_ID, -1)   @r{-1 = visible}

       ! Software sprite approach
       TYPE :: SPRITE_TYPE
           INTEGER :: X
           INTEGER :: Y
           INTEGER :: FRAME
       END TYPE SPRITE_TYPE

       TYPE(SPRITE_TYPE), DIMENSION(10) :: SPRITES

       SPRITES(I)%X = 100
       SPRITES(I)%Y = 50
       SPRITES(I)%FRAME = MOD(SPRITES(I)%FRAME + 1, 4)
@end example

@node Objects
@section Manipulating objects

Object manipulation depends on the implementation approach:

@example
       ! Using derived types
       TYPE :: GAME_OBJECT
           INTEGER :: X
           INTEGER :: Y
           INTEGER :: WIDTH
           INTEGER :: HEIGHT
           LOGICAL :: ACTIVE
       END TYPE GAME_OBJECT

       TYPE(GAME_OBJECT), DIMENSION(20) :: OBJECTS

       ! Initialize object
       OBJECTS(I)%X = 100
       OBJECTS(I)%Y = 100
       OBJECTS(I)%WIDTH = 16
       OBJECTS(I)%HEIGHT = 16
       OBJECTS(I)%ACTIVE = .TRUE.

       ! Update object position
       OBJECTS(I)%X = OBJECTS(I)%X + DX
       OBJECTS(I)%Y = OBJECTS(I)%Y + DY

       ! Check active status
       IF (OBJECTS(I)%ACTIVE) THEN
           CALL DRAW_OBJECT(OBJECTS(I))
       END IF
@end example

@node Unsupported
@section Unsupported language features

The following FORTRAN features are not supported in this EIGHTBOL release:

@table @asis
@item File I/O operations (OPEN, CLOSE, READ, WRITE, PRINT)
@item Graphics statements (PLOT, DRAW)
@item Hardware-specific features (JOYSTICK, LIGHTPEN)
@item Extended precision arithmetic (unless emulated)
@item Pointers and allocatable arrays (unless using modern Fortran)
@item Recursive subroutines (unless specified)
@item Generic procedures and interfaces
@item Object-oriented polymorphism and inheritance
@item Parallel processing constructs (FORALL, DO CONCURRENT)
@item Interoperability with C (ISO_C_BINDING)
@end table

